% ============================================================================
% symbiosis-type — three named types of symbiotic relationship and what
% actually defines each (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Two organisms living in a close, long-term relationship are sorted by ONE
% question: who benefits, and who (if anyone) is harmed? Mutualism is both
% sides winning, commensalism is one side winning while the other is
% unaffected, and parasitism is one side winning at the other's expense.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? symbiosis_type(mutualism, $D)
%       % both_parties_benefit
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `symbiosis_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a symbiosis-related term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the type it
% names:
%
%     ? symbiosis_type($T, the_parasite_benefits_while_the_host_is_harmed)
%       % parasitism
%
% The three types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type            description                                                        quote (verbatim)
%     --------------  -----------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     mutualism        both_parties_benefit                                                "Where both parties benefit, the relationship is described as mutualistic."
%     commensalism      one_organism_benefits_and_the_other_is_not_significantly_harmed_or_helped   "Commensalism describes a relationship between two living organisms where one benefits and the other is not significantly harmed or helped."
%     parasitism        the_parasite_benefits_while_the_host_is_harmed                      "In a parasitic relationship, the parasite benefits while the host is harmed."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Wikipedia's "Symbiosis" article, which
% gives each of these three types its own standalone defining sentence.
% This is a MULTI-SOURCE-STYLE table (see `ocean-current-drivers.adj`,
% `seed-dispersal-mechanism.adj`) in the sense that each row's quote comes
% from a different part of the same page rather than a single shared
% sentence — this `table`'s own `source` field carries the first row's
% (mutualism) quote; the other two rows' quotes are documented above in
% this header's truth table and are independently checkable against the
% same `locator`. WebFetch-verified across two passes before writing, the
% second pass pulling the full surrounding paragraph for each candidate
% sentence to confirm it stands alone grammatically and isn't qualified or
% contradicted by an adjacent sentence. `trust consensus` — Wikipedia, the
% same tier this stdlib already reserves for other Wikipedia citations
% (e.g. `pond-zone.adj`, `seed-dispersal-mechanism.adj`).
%
% Two related terms the SAME article's opening paragraph also names are
% deliberately NOT rows here, so a recall ABSTAINS on them rather than
% inventing a description: `competition` and `amensalism`. Both are
% real ecological interaction categories the source names, but its own
% defining sentence bundles them together as a single semicolon-joined
% compound sentence ("Where the interaction reduces both parties' fitness,
% it is called competition; where just one party's fitness is reduced, it
% is called amensalism.") rather than stating one clean fact each the way
% mutualism/commensalism/parasitism do. Picked using the mandatory
% full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline — zero hits for
% "symbiosis_type"/"mutualism"/"commensalism"/"parasitism" before this
% file was written. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table symbiosis_type {
    columns type, description

    row (mutualism, both_parties_benefit)
    row (commensalism, one_organism_benefits_and_the_other_is_not_significantly_harmed_or_helped)
    row (parasitism, the_parasite_benefits_while_the_host_is_harmed)

    source "Where both parties benefit, the relationship is described as mutualistic."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbiosis"
    trust consensus
}
